            <ArticleTitle>Heavy childhood television use persists into young adulthood and is associated with increased BMI.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>924-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/oby.21453</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To test whether TV use has both concurrent effects on body mass index (BMI) at the time of survey interviews and long-term, enduring effects via the promotion of future levels of TV use.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Two waves of survey data from the Panel Study of Income Dynamics were used to test relationships between TV use and BMI among a nationally representative sample of children and their parents (n = 1,421). Path analysis was used to model effects of childhood (1997) levels of TV use and BMI on the same variables 14 years later (2011).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Path analysis showed childhood TV use was associated positively with childhood BMI (β = 0.08, P &lt; 0.05), and young adult TV use was related positively to young adult BMI (β = 0.10, P &lt; 0.01). Heavy childhood TV use also persisted into young adulthood (β = 0.15, P &lt; 0.001), indirectly increasing young adult BMI (β = 0.02, P &lt; 0.05).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Heavy childhood TV users tend to become heavy young adult users. The continuation of TV behavior from childhood to young adulthood independently increases BMI.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2016 The Obesity Society.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
